prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 6–10 sentences. The user describes their next chunk of work like this: "I want to refactor the auth middleware, migrate the session store to a new backend, and update the API contract for our public endpoints. The migration depends on the middleware refactor (the new shape is what the migration code targets), and the API contract update has to wait for both to land because it documents the new behavior. So these need to ship in order: middleware → migration → API." How do you classify and shape this work, and what do you propose next?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers four behaviors. (1) **Recognises the work as an initiative, not one feature or three independent backlog items.** The defining signal is sequential dependency between branches that must each ship as their own CR — that's the kdevkit definition of an initiative (see SKILL.md §10). A large feature that can ship as one branch stays a feature; only when the work fans out across multiple branches in a defined order does it become an initiative. (2) **Offers to use the `start initiative <name>` entry verb** so the persistent root (Why + Streams + Status table) lives in `$SPEC_ROOT/initiative/<name>.md` and survives compaction across feature branches. The agent may briefly mention that "show initiatives" lists active ones and "stream <n> for <initiative>" is how each stream then starts as its own feature. (3) **Asks the three initiative-level interview questions** before writing the spec — Why (the realization driving this multi-stream work), Streams (the three named branches in order, each with a one-line intent and a prereq pointer), and Decisions taken at the initiative level (anything that binds all three streams — shared interfaces, naming, rollout strategy). Per-stream decisions belong in each stream's feature spec, not the initiative spec. (4) **Describes that each subsequent stream's feature spec auto-links to this initiative** — when the user later runs `stream 2 for <initiative>`, §6 Planning auto-populates the `Part of initiative: [[<name>]]` line in the new feature spec without prompting (because the initiative's Streams list names the feature unambiguously). Wrong answers: collapsing all three concerns into a single feature spec; splitting into three independent backlog items with no glue between them; jumping straight to writing a spec without offering the initiative shape; skipping the three-interview shape (Why/Streams/Decisions); failing to mention the auto-link or the persistent Status table.
tools: claude,kiro
